Important:
~~~~~~~~~~

    - Read the README file carefully; things changed.

    - NEVER RUN A TEST PROGRAM (<test>.dir/sbin/init) DIRECTLY ON YOUR
      MACHINE UNLESS YOU WROTE IT YOURSELF. PEER TESTS (all_tests, ...)
      ARE PROGRAMS WRITTEN BY OTHER PEOPLE: RUN THEM ONLY THROUGH YOUR
      KERNEL, INSIDE QEMU (make test, run_qemu, ...). A TEST THAT'S
      HARMLESS UNDER OUR KERNEL CAN DO SOMETHING VERY DIFFERENT UNDER
      LINUX; FOR EXAMPLE, A clone3 CALL OUR KERNEL REJECTS CAN BE A FORK
      UNDER LINUX, AND A LOOP OF THEM IS A FORK BOMB.
    
    - The kernel is completely yours; you can change/replace/add anything
      as long as:
           - You leave the build environment alone
           - You implement the required system calls with the correct semantics
           - The code you push is written by you + given base code on the
             master branch

Assignment:
~~~~~~~~~~~

    - Implement MAP_SHARED, the file system is still not writable
      and all file update attempts are done in the page cache (don't
      survive reboots)

    - donate a test


Defining a test case:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

<csid>.in contains what goes into stdin for init (no ^D in it:
          see "The end of stdin")
<csid>.ok contains the expected output
<csid>.dir:
    - /sbin/init [required] the statically linked, non-relocatable test program
    - /sbin/src  [nice to have] the sources and Makefile that produces
                                /sbin/init


Developing your test:
   * the best approach is to copy t0.dir, t0.in and t0.ok and change
     src/init.c
   * you can build and run your own test on any CS Linux machine (only
     yours: see "Important" at the top). You don't need a working kernel
     to write your test which is why the test deadline is not tied to
     having t0 working and is much earlier than usual


For Makefile help:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    make help

To run tests:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    make clean test

To make the output less noisy:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    make -s clean test

Server view targets:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    make get_results    # gets and displays your results
    make get_tests      # gets tests from the server
    make get_summary    # shows results per test

To run one test
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    make -s t0.test               # run the test once
    make -s t0.loop               # run the test 10 times
    LOOP_LIMIT=6 make -s t0.loop  # run the test 6 times
    make -s t0.fail               # run the test until it fails

To run by hand
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    ./run_qemu t0

To run with gdb
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    ./debug_qemu t0

       Then follow the instructions on the screen

To type input for a test by hand
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    ./console_qemu t0

       Your keyboard is the console: each key goes straight to stdin
       (Console::getch), with no echo and no line editing. ^d ends the
       input: read returns 0 from then on. The kernel's debug output and
       the console's output both print to the terminal. ^c terminates QEMU.

To run with the QEMU monitor
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    ./monitor_qemu t0

       Like run_qemu, but ^a c switches the terminal between the debug
       output and the QEMU monitor (info registers, info mem, x/..., quit).
       The console reads <test>.in, like run_qemu. ^c doesn't terminate
       QEMU here, ^a x does.

Generated files
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

For each test, a set of files will be generated:

    <test>.debug        the debug output
    <test>.time         how long it took to run the test
    <test>.failure      failure information in case of failure
    <test>.out          the lines that start with *** from the raw output
    <test>.diff         the difference between <test>.out and <test>.ok
    <test>.result       pass or fail
    <test>.data         the ext2 disk image
    build/<test>.console what the console reads: <test>.in, then ^D


